{"name":"Energetic Orange","hex":"#d85739","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"energetic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Possessing or pertaining to energy. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Characterised by force or vigour; full of energy; lively, vigorous, furious."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any material used for its release of energy, either as an explosive, propellant, or for pyrotechnic effects."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/energetic"},{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/500px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/1280px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=par